The '''Porto Vren Concession''' ({{Langx|bl|Concesion de Porto Verena}}) was a [[W:Concession (territory)|concession]] territory in [[Lacashe]] leased to the [[Baliscan Confederation|Baliscan Confederation]] under the terms of the [[Treaty of Guledga]] (1856), which concluded the [[War of Liberation (1853–1856)|War of Liberation]]. The concession, centered on the port city of [[Porto Vren]], granted Balisca administrative control, commercial privileges, and military presence in Lacashian territory for seventeen years, from 1856 until the expiration of the cotton export control provisions and the return of Porto Vren to Lacashian sovereignty in 1873.

The concession was the primary instrument through which Balisca exercised influence in post-war Lacashe. It served three functions: as the administrative center for the [[Allied occupation of Lacashe|coalition occupation forces]]; as the commercial hub through which Balisca controlled Lacashian cotton exports under the treaty's export control provisions; and as the diplomatic listening post from which Balisca monitored the volatile politics of both Lacashe and [[Libertalia]]. The concession's existence shaped the political development of both states and became a persistent source of Lacashian resentment, contributing to the revanchist politics that would dominate the country for decades after its return.

== Background ==

{{main|War of Liberation (1853–1856)|Treaty of Guledga}}

The [[War of Liberation]] (1853–1856) was fought by a coalition of powers — [[Burawa]], [[Volisania]], [[Balisca]], and [[Asikyira]] — to end [[W:Chattel slavery|chattel slavery]] in [[Lacashe]] by force. The war resulted in the decisive defeat of the Lacashian planter state and the signing of the [[Treaty of Guledga]] on 12 September 1856.

The treaty imposed several territorial and economic arrangements on Lacashe; the eastern coastal strip was split off to create [[Libertalia]], a state established for emancipated slaves and [[W:Free persons of color|free persons of color]] under the governance of the [[Freedman Congress]]. The remaining Lacashian territory was subjected to occupation by coalition forces and significant economic penalties, including [[W:Reparations|reparations]] of 75 million [[Lacashian hanti|hanti]] to be paid to Libertalia and the concession of cotton export control rights to Balisca until 1873.

The concession was carved out of Lacashian territory to provide Balisca with a temporary administrative and military base from which to enforce the treaty's provisions. The choice of [[Porto Vren]] — a border city that had served as a free port for cotton export during the embargo period — reflected both its commercial importance and its strategic position on the boundary between Lacashe and Libertalia.

== History ==
{{Main|Porto Vren#History}}

=== Return to Lacashe ===
The concession's existence was tied from the outset to the cotton export control provisions of the [[Treaty of Guledga]], which were set to expire in 1873. As the expiration date approached, the Baliscan government faced a strategic decision: negotiate an extension of the lease, or return the territory to Lacashian sovereignty. The case for retention was advanced by the Baliscan commercial interests that had profited from the export control arrangement and by the naval establishment, which valued the [[Porto Vren Naval Station]] as a strategic asset in the [[Kapuyakira Sea]] region. The case for return was made by the Confederal Council's diplomatic corps, which argued that the concession had become a political liability — a source of Lacashian resentment that undermined Balisca's broader interests in the region and provided ammunition to the revanchist [[Lacashe Freedom Society]].

The decision to return the concession was ultimately driven by the recognition that the cotton export control — the concession's primary economic rationale — could not be renewed without provoking a diplomatic crisis. The Lacashian government of the [[Trustees]] had cooperated with the concession arrangement as a condition of the occupation, but the [[Lacashe Citizen's Movement]]'s electoral victory in 1863 had brought to power a government that was explicitly hostile to the concession's continuation. Any attempt to extend the lease would have required a military confrontation that the Baliscan government was unwilling to undertake, especially with the outbreak of the [[Cantonal Revolution|Cantonal rebellions]] across Balisca in the summer of 1873 diverting much of the government's military capacity to internal issues.

The [[Porto Vren Agreement]] of 1873 provided for the orderly transfer of the concession to Lacashian sovereignty. Under the agreement, Balisca retained commercial rights in the port — including preferential docking and warehousing facilities — for a further five years. The last Baliscan Governor, [[Jordi Puigvert]], formally transferred sovereignty to the Lacashian government on 14 September 1873, the seventeenth anniversary of the Treaty of Guledga. The remaining Baliscan garrison withdrew over the following three months.

The return of the concession was celebrated by the Lacashocho population as a restoration of national sovereignty, but it was received with dread by the Lacashito community, who understood that the legal protections and social mobility they had enjoyed under Baliscan administration would not survive the transfer. The fears were well founded: within a decade, the [[Blood History Law]] of 1884 would require all residents of Lacashe to carry documentation certifying their heritage, institutionalizing the Lacashocho-Lacashito distinction that the concession had temporarily suspended.

